#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171860 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/id #uuid "06e592bb-3ebe-57f1-a282-635599780593" :question/math-academy-id "e-8753" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =  \\left\\langle ye^{xy},\\, xe^{xy}\\right\\rangle$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,$ where $C_1$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,$ where $C_2$ is the path along along the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = (1+xy)e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,2)-f(1,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\cdot 2}-{e}^{1\\cdot 1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative and $C_2$ has the same initial and terminal points as $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^{4}-e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171903 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"}] :question/id #uuid "a9f1b212-7d45-566a-95d3-fa2697ad8c3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9548" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2.$ Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r},$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 1, 0)$ to $(-1, 0, -1)$ in $\\mathbb R^3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2, 1, 0)$ and $B(-1, 0, -1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(-1,0,-1)-f(-2,1,0) \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2})-((-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-5 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171904 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/id #uuid "d659bb69-1a8d-5fb2-837c-27e454877021" :question/math-academy-id "e-9549" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x, y) = -2x\\,\\mathbf i + 6y^2\\,\\mathbf j.$ Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r},$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 0)$ to $(1, 2)$ in $\\mathbb R^2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1, 2).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 0.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = 2y^3 - x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,2)-f(-2,0) \\\\  & =(2(2)^{3}-{1}^{2})-(2(0)^{3}-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =16-1+4 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2723dc72-fd63-54a4-bb39-b1f78ca7b53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415206} {:answer/id #uuid "5c258c27-caa9-5d08-8078-126afe0392e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415217} {:answer/id #uuid "72010d15-094f-5946-8dbc-32f0cde47a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415223} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d0ed6f-03ab-58cb-b103-fad5d1a3eabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415245} {:answer/id #uuid "cc091e6f-2ffe-5090-bd21-1c547785f021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc091e6f-2ffe-5090-bd21-1c547785f021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415247} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd25f073-c3c7-52b2-85d6-ded0f2d4da84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77c64f72-bc4e-5149-99d1-c9f993fca3fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-125663" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x+y},\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8bc88c-65ec-5ed0-861b-da4a28dd2e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415207} {:answer/id #uuid "5d32c371-2553-5a69-82d8-90b81f6734fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415218} {:answer/id #uuid "6b142c8d-3eea-55c4-9289-bbf7b0018f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415221} {:answer/id #uuid "878097e9-8b52-5d48-a250-9feb84ddeb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415227} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3bdd36-68bf-5e48-b9c0-a73c00b3435d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "878097e9-8b52-5d48-a250-9feb84ddeb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415227} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6f00848-516a-51d1-8994-e5331367e330" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41c787c2-8fe3-51ae-aef3-bfbcd04677ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-125720" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(\\sqrt{2},2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2},$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\sqrt{2},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=xy.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2},2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23fa8d02-75d6-5d90-ad84-bf573bb9d9df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415204} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4b9cfd-a831-5fd0-85ab-698650ea4e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415220} {:answer/id #uuid "a86f2143-dc01-5291-af26-417281491c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415239} {:answer/id #uuid "d55a9329-8e18-5108-8eab-de793cc094fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415250} {:answer/id #uuid "da48178e-9646-5e30-ac04-7bbdb41f44dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a86f2143-dc01-5291-af26-417281491c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415239} :answer-field/id #uuid "c40c7417-b7c5-57c2-ac5e-693212f63842" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be0d9d11-b021-5cdb-982a-0da35bda23a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125727" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=4x-3y.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(1,-3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,2)$ and $B(1,-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(1,-3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(4(1)-3(-3))-(4(0)-3(2)) \\\\  & =4+9+6 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "260ed446-43fe-5beb-ab7d-78b6125c1cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5-e}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415205} {:answer/id #uuid "50955a5e-a870-5833-935b-0d75056ca130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415215} {:answer/id #uuid "52fd34b1-d7f9-5a57-9656-43e29d60efa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2{e}^{2}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415216} {:answer/id #uuid "99fc339c-7e1c-58ba-8069-a0ad53cdb041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415237} {:answer/id #uuid "a6959a00-ad55-53a1-8b0d-bd4bafcbb3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-2e}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52fd34b1-d7f9-5a57-9656-43e29d60efa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2{e}^{2}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415216} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a158f6e-8c5d-5eb1-9da0-cbf999c0636e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d068dd74-b0d6-5e4a-8ca9-75d15ab6e3ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-125733" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=y{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(\\pi ,1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,2)$ and $B(\\pi ,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(\\pi ,1)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(1){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }-(2){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}-2e \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-2e \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2{e}^{2}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036e6f03-ee36-5c5f-b814-7fbdad1c16c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415195} {:answer/id #uuid "789ac7c1-845d-586f-9c96-a1af50adc74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415225} {:answer/id #uuid "b090af4c-99bd-5025-98a2-d50859d47fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415240} {:answer/id #uuid "deac1bbc-f701-5baa-b26c-08f5a0fcebf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415253} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e8d299-71bd-546e-9566-9e1178edccba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "789ac7c1-845d-586f-9c96-a1af50adc74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415225} :answer-field/id #uuid "b08ccadb-6ec6-59d9-86d6-45aa650a0703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef46b0f0-6f4c-53dd-b0f1-5e1b301fe037" :question/math-academy-id "q-125736" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=3,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5add66-bcf6-5621-80cf-899ef43c417d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8-\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415198} {:answer/id #uuid "9549b62b-a175-541a-90f6-e4a181a146f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415234} {:answer/id #uuid "b76ff3bf-966d-5fca-9249-5a073557d751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415242} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9962c0-660e-54ff-aeb6-e838063c6839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415252} {:answer/id #uuid "edea968c-ca5f-5f22-97b5-05c3f197f4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5-\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b5add66-bcf6-5621-80cf-899ef43c417d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8-\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415198} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c832baf-abd6-548a-b77a-513f27bf73e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd1e238f-7dc4-513f-b157-39e9312d6861" :question/math-academy-id "q-125741" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡z-y.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,1)$ to $(2,-2,0)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,1)$ and $B(2,-2,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(2,-2,0)-f(1,0,1) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-(-2))-(1\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-0) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61d2add2-3ad5-5382-bacd-16dbd27b4700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415219} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6c732d-930f-5808-b25d-6b951b402fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415222} {:answer/id #uuid "7d41326b-ff6a-57f4-af5f-466b1c4babc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415226} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0f378c-b7c6-55ea-b7a0-f823f1d2518c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415230} {:answer/id #uuid "8bbbd95e-905c-5fbb-b3d2-7375a1f4547f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61d2add2-3ad5-5382-bacd-16dbd27b4700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415219} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cac645c-9b57-5ef6-9122-6b261e5ac034" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a75809b4-9c5b-5b68-8717-3a8614d8377f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125742" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x+y}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,3,1)$ to $(0,1,-1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,3,1)$ and $B(0,1,-1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,1,-1)-f(-2,3,1) \\\\  & =(-1){e}^{0+1}-(1){e}^{-2+3} \\\\  & =-e-e \\\\  & =-2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a9cbc7-f277-56ad-a34d-b1e67b9ccb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415200} {:answer/id #uuid "4199a3f5-5c75-59ad-ae31-6dbe20749db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415211} {:answer/id #uuid "7254ab02-972a-5fc3-af1e-ece583b386ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415224} {:answer/id #uuid "95a9fd1b-4296-5d3a-ad4e-a9e88dba336a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415235} {:answer/id #uuid "bed907e5-7397-570c-82dd-cbe5f3babe74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7254ab02-972a-5fc3-af1e-ece583b386ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415224} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c1d218-3b72-5245-bad5-dd08cb553d78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0027cfb5-d932-549f-8cc6-973642895e8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125743" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =e\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ed4975-6d31-5ec6-a093-5c910fbba055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415196} {:answer/id #uuid "3e05852b-fb2c-53ef-8b30-791b2b7932ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415210} {:answer/id #uuid "450c00f1-3112-5f7e-b618-7f3d64cf06cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415213} {:answer/id #uuid "c98cff8e-42c5-5b37-a1b7-f50921ee92cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415246} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3ee363-bc3b-5b3b-99ff-bea41f6d77d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c98cff8e-42c5-5b37-a1b7-f50921ee92cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415246} :answer-field/id #uuid "d67c0c93-91d0-53c7-a667-f86c44dece23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "975a843e-4bff-52d8-aa59-728678109c63" :question/math-academy-id "q-125747" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=6xy\\,i+3{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0)$ and $B(2,-2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=3{x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,-2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0625084b-e9ca-5f9d-9edb-7cd203c49e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256835} {:answer/id #uuid "2332677c-db38-5732-86a6-edbc38915c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257076} {:answer/id #uuid "767cc64e-5a0a-54f8-b56e-81ea5d23171a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257822} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcf9ffb-d8c0-51f0-a661-b433dc2b60e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257878} {:answer/id #uuid "e0403918-536a-524a-8c14-ab0744be6702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2332677c-db38-5732-86a6-edbc38915c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257076} :answer-field/id #uuid "83b90cfc-e328-5b69-bfa9-ca822accdfd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "157b91e2-9d0a-5ac1-ac9e-d1e250dcce18" :question/math-academy-id "q-125748" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,-1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(1,-1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,-1)-f(-1,1) \\\\  & =(-1){e}^{{1}^{2}}-(1){e}^{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-e-e \\\\  & =-2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a6ae28-ab05-53de-9b3f-5e347ef15023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415194} {:answer/id #uuid "14b4d724-583b-5a37-a444-101bb4a57ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415199} {:answer/id #uuid "32debd00-39de-5d6e-9e96-d38fb37c9e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415208} {:answer/id #uuid "485effef-da1a-51e4-aa09-f8d8650ea3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415214} {:answer/id #uuid "d067cbee-9b2c-5c36-8dc8-c47522a4af35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14b4d724-583b-5a37-a444-101bb4a57ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415199} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ad03362-7885-5567-9b35-4c8299722802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c651ce1-2ea6-5480-b7a8-389903a354b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-125750" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+2y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(\\sqrt{3},2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(\\sqrt{3},2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={y}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{3},2)-f(-1,1) \\\\  & =(2)^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-(1)^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f497857-d2ea-515b-ac70-f83db9b8d663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415202} {:answer/id #uuid "394650d1-8e19-51ba-a71f-b0fc64e540e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415209} {:answer/id #uuid "88f09cc1-4f58-5190-94f6-808435cc1c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415228} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc0005d-5201-5c11-8909-40e11ed0a852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415248} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f0d68b-8c4e-509f-990d-cfc2226353ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0f0d68b-8c4e-509f-990d-cfc2226353ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415254} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5443ff1-90ef-5376-a9ca-4302c15cea52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13b80d0a-868f-5180-a5b8-148925e3a0e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125756" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(2,0)$ to $(3,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(2,0)$ and $B(3,-2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,-2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+1}-2\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09235254-4d47-5c19-a8cc-f2c8d86165e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415197} {:answer/id #uuid "43de946b-6276-5f5e-981d-3f9e49ef0b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415212} {:answer/id #uuid "991eb7ba-c62e-5f6d-b399-c7f8b3631d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415236} {:answer/id #uuid "c2db9fc1-1412-5d23-8af2-c825674e2420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415244} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b50554-1538-5e80-9bff-964961a87a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09235254-4d47-5c19-a8cc-f2c8d86165e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415197} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeed41f2-bb2a-5596-8fa5-7363fbf67f7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2675f4f-103b-59c5-b57c-1f769858716e" :question/math-academy-id "q-125759" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0)$ to $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0)$ and $B(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi /2)}-1{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =2{e}^{1}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d16442d-19e4-582c-8e49-f56bc5ac7989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419011} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4765c6-63f6-569c-bd76-1517cf62b38c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419012} {:answer/id #uuid "91c67dbf-ae46-5cea-83af-1664e56a1941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419013} {:answer/id #uuid "eb70dc47-8da4-592d-92b1-cbf8738afc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419014} {:answer/id #uuid "fea9db8f-ed00-5f80-923f-1ea3dcb8fe89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d16442d-19e4-582c-8e49-f56bc5ac7989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419011} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6809d48-fda2-518b-8b59-a579c69de413" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b47df0de-b5ee-5562-b36a-a659cbe48d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-125796" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,x⟩$ and the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨0,\\,1-2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2t-1,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17551007-968c-50bb-8713-508a56887419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415201} {:answer/id #uuid "21edad9a-788d-531c-a881-1f0e31295a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415203} {:answer/id #uuid "89ae9690-9dcf-58c2-8b46-641ef0e6072e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415229} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4ab0a1-3455-57e5-b1fe-fa7e38dc4456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415233} {:answer/id #uuid "b159a7f5-1313-51eb-887e-62e29469c644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ae9690-9dcf-58c2-8b46-641ef0e6072e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415229} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b140e5-29aa-510b-86ab-1d425008b9cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f45f3fc-c4df-53d5-b37f-c7ba8162617a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98951" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=xyz+{y}^{2}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,-1)$ to $(2,1,1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,-1)$ and $B(2,1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(2,1,1)-f(1,0,-1) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1+{1}^{2})-(1\\cdot 0\\cdot (-1)+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
